DTSTAMP:20260930T221358Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Former union officials charged with wire frau
 d\, conspiracy to commit wire fraud for embezzling approximately $350K in 
 member funds over 5 years
DESCRIPTION:CLEVELAND – An investigation by the U.S. Department of Labor
  has led to criminal charges for two former union officials who allegedly 
 embezzled approximately $350\,000 from the union members they swore to ser
 ve.An investigation by the department’s Office of Labor-Management Stand
 ards in Cleveland found that the former president and secretary-treasurer 
 of the American Federation of State\, County\, and Municipal Employees Loc
 al 1746 conspired to access the union’s finances for unauthorized transa
 ctions for their personal benefit from 2017 to 2022.Criminal charges were 
 filed this month in the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of O
 hio against the union’s former President Pamela Brown and former Secreta
 ry-Treasurer Adelle Burrell-Williams. The charging documents allege these 
 former officers used the union’s debit card to withdraw cash at casinos 
 and wrote unauthorized checks from the union’s operating account. The de
 partment also alleges that Brown and Burrell-Williams attempted to avoid d
 etection by creating false reports and failing to maintain financial recor
 ds. The union lost approximately $350\,000 as a result of Brown and Burrel
 l-Williams’ actions.  The former union officials were responsible for o
 verseeing the union\, managing its finances\, paying authorized expenses\,
  maintaining financial records\, and creating financial reports during the
 ir tenures as principal officers of Local 1746\, a union representing 1\,2
 00 employees at Cuyahoga County Health and Human Services.The prosecution 
 of this case is being handled by the U.S. Attorney’s Office for the Nort
 hern District of Ohio.Criminal complaints\, or the filing of an informatio
 n\, are charges only and are not evidence of guilt. Each defendant is enti
 tled to a fair trial in which it is the government’s burden to prove gui
 lt beyond a reasonable doubt.  Labor union fraud such as embezzlement\, f
 iling false reports\, maintaining false records\, destroying or concealing
  records\, and other criminal activity can be repo\nhttp://www.dol.gov/new
 sroom/releases/olms/olms20260930
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/olms/olms20260930
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f211cd62bbfcaa02@role-professional-responsibility.maine-government-fee
 ds
DTSTAMP:20260929T221404Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Requirements for complaint for probable-cause
  determinations
DESCRIPTION:STATE OF MAINE \nSUPREME JUDICIAL COURT \nAMENDMENT TO \nMAINE
  RULES OF UNIFIED CRIMINAL PROCEDURE \n \n2026 Me. Rules 03 \n \nEffective
 : October 1\, 2026 \n \n All of the Justices concurring therein\, the foll
 owing amendment to the \nMaine Rules of Unified Criminal Procedure is adop
 ted to be effective on the date \nindicated above.  The specific amendment
   is stated below.  To aid in the \nunderstanding of the amendment\, an Ad
 visory Note appears after the text of \nthe amendment.  The Advisory Note 
 states the reason for recommending the \namendment\, but the Advisory Note
  is not part of the amendment adopted by \nthe Court. \n \n 1. Rule 4A of 
 the  Maine Rules of Unified Criminal  Procedure is \namended to read as fo
 llows: \n \nRULE 4A.  PROBABLE CAUSE DETERMINATION UPON WARRANTLESS \nARRE
 ST FOR ANY CRIME \n \n(a) Timing: Required Findings.   Except in a bona fi
 de emergency or \nother extraordinary circumstance\, when a defendant arre
 sted without a \nwarrant for any crime is not released from custody within
  48 hours after arrest\, \nincluding Saturdays\, Sundays\, and legal holid
 ays\, the court or justi ce of the \npeace shall determine\, within that t
 ime period\, whether there is probable cause \nto believe that a crime has
  been committed and that the arrested defendant has \ncommitted it.  If th
 e evidence does not establish such probable cause\, the court \nor jus tic
 e of the peace shall discharge the arrested defendant.  If a probable \nca
 use determination has not taken place within 36 hours after the arrest\, \
 nincluding Saturdays\, Sundays\, and legal holidays\, the custodian shall 
 notify the \nattorney for the State of the upcoming deadline.  For purpose
 s of this Rule \n“custody” means incarceration. Rule 45(a) and (b) hav
 e no application to this \nsubdivision. \n(b) Evidence.  In making this de
 termination the court or justice of the \npeace shall consider:  \n 2 \n(1
 ) the sworn complaint made in compliance with Rule 3(b)\; \n(2) an affidav
 it or affidavits\, if any\, filed by the State\; \n(3) a sworn oral statem
 ent o\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/rules/amendments/2026_mr_03_crim_2026-
 09-24.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/rules/amendments/2026_mr_03_crim_2026-09-
 24.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
